place), so 28,620.\n\nThen the hundreds digit: 2×477=954, shifted two positions left, so 95,400.\n\nAdding them up as before gives the same total. So I think that's correct. So the final answer should be 125,451.\n\nWait a second, just to make sure, maybe I can do a quick estimation. 477 is approximately 500, and 263 is approximately 260. 500×260=130,000. Our result is 125,451, which is a bit less, which makes sense because both numbers were rounded up. So that seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, let me check with another method: using (a+b)(c+d) but not sure if that helps. Alternatively, maybe use calculator steps.\n\nAlternatively, cross-verify with another multiplication approach:\n\nLet me compute 477×263.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n477 × 263 = 477 × (200 + 60 +3) = 477×200 +477×60 +477×3.\n\nWait, that's exactly what I did. So the calculations seem consistent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps compute 263 ×477 step by step.\n\nWait, let me try 263×400 = 105,200.\n\n263×70 = 18,410.\n\n263×7 = 1,841.\n\nAdding those together: 105,200 +18,410 = 123,610 +1,841 = 125,451. Oh, that's another way and also gives the same result. So that's a good check. So yes, the answer should be 125,451. I think that's correct.\n\nSo the reasoning steps would be as per the template. Let me just structure it as per the user's example.\n\nThe original question had number1=27 and number2=153. The example reasoning steps were:\n\n1. Multiply 27 by 3 (ones place) =81\n\n2. Multiply 27 by 50 (tens place) =1350\n\n3. Multiply 27 by 100 (hundreds place)=2700\n\nThen sum: 81+1350=1431+2700=4131.\n\nSo following that structure, for 477×263:\n\nNumber1 is 477, number2 is 263 (which is 200+60+3). So the steps would be:\n\n1. Multiply 477 by 3 (ones place digit 3) =1,431\n\n2. Multiply 477 by 60 (tens place digit 50 in the example, here 60) =28,620\n\n3. Multiply 477 by 200 (hundreds place digit 200) =95,400\n\nThen sum all three:1,431 +28,620 +95,400 =125,451.\n\nTherefore the answer is 125,451. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations now.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 477 and 263 is \\boxed{125451}.\n"]  1%|1         | 7/500 [06:18<7:02:40, 51.44s/it]
