lace)\n2. Multiply 970 by 30 (tens place)\n3. Multiply 970 by 100 (hundreds place)\n\nThen sum all three results. \n\nTherefore, in the template's reasoning, the steps would be written as:\n\n1. Multiply 970 by 8 (the ones digit of 138)\n2. Multiply 970 by 30 (the tens digit, which is 3*10)\n3. Multiply 970 by 100 (the hundreds digit, which is 1*100)\n\nThen sum those three products. \n\nSo the answer should be presented in that structure. I think that's how the example did it. Let me check the example again.\n\nIn the example, the number2 was 153, so the steps were:\n\n1. Multiply by 3 (ones)\n2. Multiply by 50 (tens)\nThen presumably a third step for the hundreds place (100). But in the original template, the user wrote \"other digits of number2 if it has\" but in the example, they had three digits, so three steps. \n\nTherefore, in this case, the reasoning should have three steps: ones, tens, hundreds. \n\nTherefore, the answer would be structured as follows:\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 138 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.\n1. Multiply 970 by the ones place digit 8 of 138: 970 \u00d7 8 = 7,760\n2. Multiply 970 by the tens place digit 30 of 138: 970 \u00d7 30 = 29,100\n3. Multiply 970 by the hundreds place digit 100 of 138: 970 \u00d7 100 = 97,000\nNow, sum all the step results: 7,760 + 29,100 + 97,000 = 133,860\nSo, the final computed product is 133,860.\n\nWait, but in the original template example, the steps were written as:\n\n1. Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153\n2. Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153\n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>\n\nSo, in the problem with 138, since it has hundreds place, we need to include a third step. \n\nTherefore, the answer should have three steps. \n\nI think that's correct. Therefore, the reasoning section would be as above. \n\nI think that's the correct way. Let me just confirm once again the calculations to be safe. \n\n970 \u00d7 138:\n\nBreakdown:\n\nFirst partial product: 970 \u00d7 8 = 7,760\n\nSecond partial product: 970 \u00d7 30 = 29,100\n\nThird partial product: 970 \u00d7 100 = 97,000\n\nAdding them together:\n\n7,760 + 29,100 = 36,860\n\n36,860 + 97,000 = 133,860\n\nYes, that's correct. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is 133,860. I think this is correct. I can't see any mistakes here. So the template should be filled out as such.\n</think>\n\n",
