e-digit number, so 518 has three digits, so three steps. So in the reasoning, the user's example had:\n\n1. Multiply by the ones digit (3 in 153)\n2. Multiply by the tens digit (50 in 153)\n3. Multiply by the hundreds digit (100 in 153)\nSo in this case, 518's digits are 5 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, actually, the digits are 5 (hundreds), 1 (tens), and 8 (ones). So the steps would be:\n\n1. Multiply 629 by 8 (ones place)\n2. Multiply 629 by 10 (tens place, since the tens digit is 1)\n3. Multiply 629 by 500 (hundreds place, since the hundreds digit is 5)\n\nSo the steps are exactly as I did. So the sum is 5032 + 6290 + 314500 = 325,822. So that's correct. So the final answer is 325,822.\n\nI think that's solid. No mistakes detected in the steps. So the answer should be 325,822.\n\n</think>\n\n",
