 = 532, but shifted one position to the left: 5320.\n\nThird, multiply by 6 (hundreds place, so 600):\n\n532 ×6 = 3192, shifted two positions: 319200.\n\nNow add them:\n\n4788\n\n+5320\n\n=10,108\n\nThen 10,108 + 319,200 = 329,308. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check with another method, like using distributive property:\n\n532 × 619 = 532 × (600 + 10 +9) = 532×600 + 532×10 +532×9, which is exactly what I did. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using a calculator would be better, but since I can't, maybe I can compute 532 × 600 = 319,200, which is correct. Then 532 ×19 = ?\n\nWait, maybe breaking down 619 as 600 + 19? Let me see:\n\n532 ×619 =532×(600 + 19)= 532×600 +532×19. \n\n532×600 is 319,200. \n\n532×19: 532×20=10,640 minus 532 gives 10,640 -532 = 10,108. So total is 319,200 +10,108 =329,308. Same result. So that confirms it.\n\nTherefore, the product should be 329,308. \n\nWait, but let me just ensure there's no arithmetic mistake. Let me recalculate 532 × 19:\n\n532 ×19 =532×(20-1)=532×20 -532 =10,640 -532 =10,108. Yes, correct.\n\nAlternatively, 532×10=5,320; 532×9=4,788. Adding 5,320 +4,788 =10,108. Same as before. \n\nSo the total is indeed 329,308. \n\nHmm, I think that's correct. I don't see any errors in the steps. So the final answer should be 329,308.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 532 and 619 is \\boxed{329308}.\n"]
