final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 571 and 275, we break down the multiplication into parts based on the digits of 275 and their place values:\n\n1. **Multiply 571 by the ones place digit (5):**\n   \\[\n   571 \\times 5 = 2855\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 571 by the tens place digit (70):**\n   \\[\n   571 \\times 70 = 571 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 3997 \\times 10 = 39970\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 571 by the hundreds place digit (200):**\n   \\[\n   571 \\times 200 = 571 \\times 2 \\times 100 = 1142 \\times 100 = 114200\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum the results of these multiplications:\n\\[\n2855 + 39970 + 114200\n\\]\n\nFirst, add the results of the first two steps:\n\\[\n2855 + 39970 = 42825\n\\]\n\nThen add the result of the third step:\n\\[\n42825 + 114200 = 157025\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 571 and 275 is \\(\\boxed{157025}\\).', "157,025?\n\nWait, let me check: 42,825 + 114,200. 42,825 + 100,000 = 142,825. Then add 14,200 more: 142,825 +14,200 = 157,025. So total is 157,025.\n\nWait, but let me verify this with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the multiplication steps. Let me try multiplying 571 × 275 in another way.\n\nAlternatively, use the standard algorithm:\n\n       571\n     ×275\n     --------\nFirst multiply by 5 (ones place):\n\n5 × 571 = 2855.\n\nThen multiply by 7 (tens place, so shifted one position to the left):\n\n7 × 571 = 3997 → but shifted one place becomes 39970.\n\nThen multiply by 2 (hundreds place, shifted two places):\n\n2 × 571 = 1142 → shifted two places becomes 114200.\n\nAdding them: 2855 + 39970 = 42825; 42825 + 114200 = 157,025. That matches.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method: 571 × 275.\n\nAlternatively, 275 is 200 +70 +5, so the same as above.\n\nAlternatively, check using calculator-like approach:\n\nLet me compute 571 × 275.\n\nFirst, 500 × 200 = 100,000.\n\nWait, but that's breaking into (500 +70 +1) × (200 +70 +5). That might be too complicated, but perhaps cross-verify with another way.\n\nAlternatively, 571 × 275 = 571 × (250 +25) = 571×250 + 571×25.\n\nCompute 571×250: 571×25×10.\n\n571×25: 571×20 = 11,420 and 571×5=2,855. So total 11,420 +2,855 =14,275. Then ×10 =142,750.\n\nThen 571×25: Wait, no, wait. Wait, 275 is 250+25? Yes. So 571×250 is 571×25×10. Wait, but maybe better to do 571×250 is 571×25 ×10. Let me recast:\n\nWait, 250 is 25×10, so 571×250 is (571×25) ×10. Let me compute 571×25 first. 571×25 = (500×25) + (70×25)+(1×25) = 12,500 +1,750 +25 = 14,275. Then ×10 gives 142,750.\n\nThen 571×25 is the remaining part. Wait, no, actually, the original split is 275=250+25, so 571×250 +571×25. Wait, but 250+25=275? 250+25 is 275. Yes. So total would be 142,750 (from 250) plus 571×25 (which is 14,275). So 142,750 +14,275 = 157,025. Same result. So that confirms it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps check with another method, like 571 × 275 = (500 +71) ×275 = 500×275 +71×275.\n\nCompute 500×275 = 137,500. Then 71×275. Let's compute 70×275 +1×275.\n\n70×275: 70×200=14,000; 70×75=5,250. So total 14,000 +5,250=19,250. Then +275 gives 19,250 +275=19,525. So total is 137,500 +19,525=157,025. Same answer again. So that's good.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct product is 157,025. Let me just make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors in my steps above. The key steps all add up, so I think that's right.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 571 and 275 is \\boxed{157025}.\n"]
