,080 +28=1,108). Then \u00d7100 gives 110,800. Correct.\n\nAdding all three: 1,385 +19,390=20,775, then +110,800=131,575. So that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is correct. I don't see any mistakes in the steps. The key steps were breaking down the multiplication into each digit's place value, computing each partial product, then adding them all together. Each step checked out when I re-verified. The approximation also makes sense, so I'm confident the final answer is 131,575.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 277 and 475 is \\boxed{131575}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 277 and 475?\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. Since 475 has three digits, we can break the multiplication into three steps.\n\n1. Multiply 277 by the ones place digit (5) of 475:\n   \\[\n   277 \\times 5 = 1385\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 277 by the tens place digit (70) of 475:\n   \\[\n   277 \\times 70 = 19390\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 277 by the hundreds place digit (400) of 475:\n   \\[\n   277 \\times 400 = 110800\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n1385 + 19390 + 110800 = 131575\n\\]\n\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: \\(1385 + 19390 + 110800 = 131575\\).\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{131575}.",
