ybe compute using another method. Let's do 141 × 636.\n\nAlternatively, break down 636 as 600 + 30 +6, so:\n\n141*(600 +30 +6) =141*600 +141*30 +141*6, which is exactly the same as before, so same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n       141  \n     ×636  \n     --------\n        846   (141×6)\n       4230   (141×30, shifted one position)\n      84600   (141×600, shifted two positions)\n     --------\n      89676\n\nAdding those up:\n\n846 + 4230 = 5076; 5076 + 84,600 = 89,676. So the total is 89,676. \n\nHmm, that seems correct. Let me try another way, maybe using distributive property differently.\n\nAlternatively, 141 ×636 can be written as (140 +1) ×636 =140×636 +1×636.\n\nCompute 140×636: 140×600 =84,000; 140×36=5,040. So 84,000 +5,040 =89,040. Then add 1×636=636. So total is 89,040 +636 =89,676. That matches.\n\nAlternatively, 636 ×141. Let's see:\n\n636 ×100 =63,600\n\n636 ×40 =25,440\n\n636 ×1 =636\n\nAdding those: 63,600 +25,440 =89,040; plus 636 gives 89,676. Same answer. So that's good.\n\nTherefore, I think the product is 89,676.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 141 and 636 is \\boxed{89676}.\n"]
