t's correct.\n\nTherefore, the product of 160 and 855 is 136,800.\n\nWait, but in the template, the user wants the reasoning to be broken down into steps where each digit of number2 (which is 855 here) is multiplied by number1 (160). Since 855 has three digits, the reasoning should have three steps. Let me structure it accordingly as per the problem's example.\n\nOriginal example for 27\u00d7153:\n\n1. Multiply 27 by 3 (ones place)\n2. Multiply 27 by 50 (tens place)\n3. Multiply 27 by 100 (hundreds place)\nThen sum all three.\n\nSo applying that to 160 \u00d7855:\n\n1. Multiply 160 by 5 (ones place of 855)\n2. Multiply 160 by 50 (tens place)\n3. Multiply 160 by 800 (hundreds place)\nThen sum all three.\n\nSo the steps would be:\n\n1. 160 \u00d75 = 800\n\n2. 160 \u00d750 = 8,000\n\n3. 160 \u00d7800 = 128,000\n\nAdding those: 800 +8,000 = 8,800; then +128,000 gives 136,800. So that's exactly as above.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 136,800. I think that's solid. Let me check with calculator math.\n\nWait, I can do 160 \u00d7 855:\n\nFirst, 160 \u00d7 800 = 128,000\n\n160 \u00d755 = 160\u00d7(50+5)= 8,000 +800=8,800\n\nSo total 128,000 +8,800 =136,800. Yes, same result.\n\nTherefore, confident that the answer is correct. The reasoning steps are as outlined.\n</think>\n\n",
