od to ensure I didn't make a mistake in the initial steps. Maybe using the standard algorithm.\n\nLet me multiply 647 × 596 using the standard method.\n\nFirst, write 647\n\n×596\n\n------------\n\nStart with the ones digit: 6 × 647. Which is 3,882 (as before).\n\nThen the tens digit: 9 (which is 90) × 647. So 9 × 647 = 5,823, then shift one position left: 58,230 (matches earlier).\n\nThen the hundreds digit: 5 (which is 500) × 647. 5 × 647 = 3,235, then shift two positions left: 323,500 (same as before). Adding them gives 3,882 + 58,230 = 62,112; 62,112 + 323,500 = 385,612. So that seems consistent.\n\nBut let me check with another approach, perhaps using the difference of squares or another method? Wait, 647 × 596. Hmm, maybe not straightforward. Alternatively, use calculator-like steps. Wait, perhaps I can compute 647 × 600 first, then subtract 647 ×4. Let's try that.\n\n647 × 600 = 647 × 6 × 100 = 3,882 × 100 = 388,200.\n\nThen 647 ×4 = 2,588.\n\nSo 388,200 - 2,588 = ?\n\n388,200 - 2,000 = 386,200\n\nThen subtract 588 more: 386,200 - 588 = 385,612. Same result! Okay, so that confirms it. So the product is indeed 385,612.\n\nTherefore, the steps are correct and the answer is 385,612.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 647 and 596 is \\boxed{385612}.\n\n\nTo find the product of 647 and 596, we break down 596 into its place values (500, 90, and 6) and multiply each part by 647, then sum the results.\n\n1. Multiply 647 by 6 (ones place):\n   \\[\n   647 \\times 6 = 3882\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 647 by 90 (tens place):\n   \\[\n   647 \\times 90 = 647 \\times 9 \\times 10 = 5823 \\times 10 = 58230\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 647 by 500 (hundreds place):\n   \\[\n   647 \\times 500 = 647 \\times 5 \\times 100 = 3235 \\times 100 = 323500\n   \\]\n\nSumming these results:\n\\[\n3882 + 58230 + 323500 = 385612\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 647 and 596 is \\(\\boxed{385612}\\)."]
