ent approaches led to the same result, so that's reassuring. I think that's solid. \n\nJust to recap the original step-by-step as per the template: \n\n1. Multiply 530 by 6 (the ones place) to get 3,180.\n\n2. Multiply 530 by 70 (the tens place) to get 37,100.\n\n3. Multiply 530 by 900 (the hundreds place) to get 477,000.\n\nAdding them all together: 3,180 + 37,100 = 40,280; then 40,280 + 477,000 = 517,280. \n\nYep, that's exactly what I did. So the answer should be 517,280. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations here. All the cross-checking methods align, so I think that's the correct product.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 530 and 976 is \\boxed{517280}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 530 and 976?\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 976 has three digits, so we can break it down into its place values and multiply each part separately.\n\n1. **Multiply 530 by the ones place digit (6):**\n   \\[\n   530 \\times 6 = 3180\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 530 by the tens place digit (70):**\n   \\[\n   530 \\times 70 = 37100\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 530 by the hundreds place digit (900):**\n   \\[\n   530 \\times 900 = 477000\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n3180 + 37100 + 477000 = 517280\n\\]\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{517280}.",
