eps with 27\u00d7153, so maybe they expect the same step-by-step breakdown here. Let me structure it exactly as per the example:\n\nFirst, multiplying by the ones digit (1), then the tens (60), then the hundreds (900). Then sum all three. So following that structure:\n\n1. 133 \u00d7 1 = 133\n\n2. 133 \u00d7 60 = 7,980\n\n3. 133 \u00d7 900 = 119,700\n\nAdding those: 133 + 7,980 = 8,113; then 8,113 + 119,700 = 127,813. So the final answer is 127,813. Alright, that seems solid.\n\nI think that's correct. Let me just check once more. 133 \u00d7 961. Let me do 130 \u00d7 961 plus 3\u00d7961.\n\n130 \u00d7 961: 100\u00d7961 = 96,100; 30\u00d7961 = 28,830. Adding those gives 96,100 + 28,830 = 124,930.\n\nThen 3\u00d7961 = 2,883. Adding to 124,930 + 2,883 = 127,813. Yep, same answer. Definitely correct. Okay, confident now.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 133 and 961 is \\boxed{127813}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 133 and 961?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 961 has three digits, so we can break it down into hundreds, tens, and ones places.\n1. Multiply 133 by the ones place digit (1) of 961:\n   \\[\n   133 \\times 1 = 133\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 133 by the tens place digit (60) of 961:\n   \\[\n   133 \\times 60 = 133 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 798 \\times 10 = 7980\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 133 by the hundreds place digit (900) of 961:\n   \\[\n   133 \\times 900 = 133 \\times 9 \\times 100 = 1197 \\times 100 = 119700\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n133 + 7980 + 119700 = 133 + 7980 = 8113\n\\]\n\\[\n8113 + 119700 = 127813\n\\]\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{127813}.",
