oning are correct. I think that's solid. I just need to present it in the template as specified.\n\nWait, the user's example had three steps for 153, so for 372 (three digits), the reasoning should have three steps. Let me check the original problem's template:\n\nOriginal example for 27 and 153:\n\n1. Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153  \n2. Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153  \n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>  \nAnswer: Now, sum all the step results: <<sum of the results>>.  \nSo, the final computed product is <<answer>>.\n\nWait, in the example, the second step was multiplying by 50 (the tens place digit 5, so 5*10), so similarly for 372, the hundreds place is 3 (so 3*100), tens is 7 (7*10), ones is 2.\n\nTherefore, the steps should be:\n\n1. Multiply 318 by 2 (ones place)  \n2. Multiply 318 by 70 (tens place)  \n3. Multiply 318 by 300 (hundreds place)  \n\nThen sum all three. So in the reasoning section, it should have those three steps. The answer is the sum. So the user wants each step to correspond to each digit's place value. So the reasoning should follow that structure. Let me make sure I present that in the template correctly.\n\nYes, so the reasoning should be written as three steps:\n\n1. Multiply 318 by 2 (ones place digit 2 of 372)  \n2. Multiply 318 by 70 (tens place digit 7 of 372, so 7*10)  \n3. Multiply 318 by 300 (hundreds place digit 3 of 372, so 3*100)  \n\nThen sum those three results (636 +22,260 +95,400 =118,296). So the final answer is 118,296.\n\nI think that's all. I don't see any mistakes in the calculations, and cross-verified with different methods. So the answer should be correct.\n</think>\n\n",
