might be more complicated. Alternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm again.\n\nAlternatively, compute 873 × 387 step by step again to confirm.\n\nFirst step: 873 ×7 = 6111. Correct.\n\nSecond step: 873 ×80 = 69840. Correct.\n\nThird step: 873 ×300 = 261900. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 6111 +69840 =75951; 75951 +261900 =337,851. Seems consistent.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method. Let's do 873 × 387.\n\nAlternatively, using the formula (a+b)(c+d) but not sure. Alternatively, maybe compute 800×300 + 800×80 + 800×7 + 70×300 etc. Maybe too time-consuming.\n\nAlternatively, using calculator-like approach: 873 ×387.\n\nLet me compute 873 × 400 = 349,200. Then subtract 873 ×13 = 11,349.\n\nSo 349,200 - 11,349 = ?\n\n349,200 - 10,000 = 339,200\n\n339,200 -1,349 = 337,851. Yes, same result. So that's a good check.\n\nTherefore, the product should be 337,851. I think that's correct.\n\nWait, but let me re-express the original partial products again to be thorough:\n\nFirst partial product (units place 7): 873 ×7 =6,111.\n\nSecond partial product (tens place 8, which is 80): 873×80=69,840.\n\nThird partial product (hundreds place 3, which is 300): 873×300=261,900.\n\nAdding all three:\n\n6,111 + 69,840 = 75,951.\n\n75,951 +261,900 = 337,851. Yep, that's the same.\n\nSo, all steps check out. I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 873 and 387 is \\boxed{337851}.\n"]
