50 is 5\u00d710, so 129\u00d75=645, then \u00d710=6,450. Correct.\n\nThird partial product (hundreds place):\n\n129 \u00d7900. 900 is 9\u00d7100. So 129\u00d79 first:\n\n129\u00d79. Let's compute again:\n\n100\u00d79=900, 20\u00d79=180, 9\u00d79=81. 900+180=1,080; 1,080+81=1,161. Then \u00d7100 gives 116,100. Correct.\n\nSo adding 645 +6,450=7,095; then 7,095+116,100=123,195. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like (130 -1) \u00d7955 to see if it's easier?\n\nLet me try that. 129 is 130 -1. So (130 -1)*955 =130\u00d7955 -1\u00d7955.\n\nCalculating 130\u00d7955: 100\u00d7955=95,500; 30\u00d7955=28,650. Adding them: 95,500 +28,650=124,150. Then subtract 955: 124,150 -955=?\n\n124,150 minus 955: Let's do 124,150 - 1,000 =123,150, then add back 45 (since I subtracted 45 more than needed). So 123,150 +45=123,195. Which matches the previous result. So that's a good check.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 123,195. I think that's correct. I don't see any mistakes in the calculations now.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 129 and 955 is \\boxed{123195}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 129 and 955, we break down the multiplication into steps based on the place values of the digits in 955 (900, 50, and 5).\n\n1. **Multiply 129 by the ones place digit (5):**\n   \\[\n   129 \\times 5 = 645\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 129 by the tens place digit (50):**\n   \\[\n   129 \\times 50 = 129 \\times 5 \\times 10 = 645 \\times 10 = 6450\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 129 by the hundreds place digit (900):**\n   \\[\n   129 \\times 900 = 129 \\times 9 \\times 100\n   \\]\n   First, calculate \\(129 \\times 9\\):\n   \\[\n   129 \\times 9 = 1161\n   \\]\n   Then multiply by 100:\n   \\[\n   1161 \\times 100 = 116100\n   \\]\n\n**Sum all the partial products:**\n\\[\n645 + 6450 + 116100\n\\]\n\n- Adding the first two results:\n  \\[\n  645 + 6450 = 7095\n  \\]\n\n- Adding the result to the third partial product:\n  \\[\n  7095 + 116100 = 123195\n  \\]\n