\nThen adding all three results: 4554 + 20,240 + 101,200.\n\nLet me compute that again step by step:\n\nFirst, 4554 + 20,240:\n\n4554 + 20,000 = 24,554\n\n24,554 + 240 = 24,794.\n\nThen add 101,200:\n\n24,794 + 100,000 = 124,794\n\n124,794 + 1,200 = 125,994.\n\nYes, so that's correct.\n\nSo the answer is 125,994. I need to present the reasoning in the template's format, following the steps as per the example.\n\nWait, the example had:\n\n1. Multiply by the ones digit (3) \u2192 27\u00d73=81\n\n2. Multiply by the tens digit (50) \u2192 27\u00d750=1350\n\nThen answer was sum of 81 +1350 + 2700 (from hundreds digit 100) \u2192 total 4131.\n\nSo following that structure, for 506\u00d7249:\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Multiply 506 by the ones digit (9): 506\u00d79=4554\n\n2. Multiply 506 by the tens digit (40): 506\u00d740=20,240\n\n3. Multiply 506 by the hundreds digit (200): 506\u00d7200=101,200\n\nThen sum all three: 4554 +20,240 +101,200 = 125,994.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning should list each step as multiplying by each digit's place value, then sum them up.\n\nSo the final answer is 125,994.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 506 and 249?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 249 has three digits, so we can break it into three steps.  \n1. Multiply 506 by the ones place digit 9 of 249:  \n   \\( 506 \\times 9 = 4,\\!554 \\).  \n2. Multiply 506 by the tens place digit 40 of 249:  \n   \\( 506 \\times 40 = 20,\\!240 \\).  \n3. Multiply 506 by the hundreds place digit 200 of 249:  \n   \\( 506 \\times 200 = 101,\\!200 \\).  \nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\( 4,\\!554 + 20,\\!240 + 101,\\!200 = 125,\\!994 \\).  \nSo, the final computed product is **125,994**.",
