final_output:  ["\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 379 and 418?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 418 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.\n1. Multiply 379 by the ones place digit 8 of 418: \\(379 \\times 8 = 3032\\).\n2. Multiply 379 by the tens place digit 10 of 418: \\(379 \\times 10 = 3790\\).\n3. Multiply 379 by the hundreds place digit 400 of 418: \\(379 \\times 400 = 151600\\).\n\nNow, sum all the step results: \\(3032 + 3790 + 151600\\).\n\nFirst, add \\(3032\\) and \\(3790\\):\n\\[\n3032 + 3790 = 6822\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to \\(151600\\):\n\\[\n6822 + 151600 = 158422\n\\]\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\(158422\\).\n\n# Answer:\nThe product of 379 and 418 is \\boxed{158422}.", '158,422.\n\nWait, but in the example given in the problem statement (the first one with 27 and 153), the reasoning mentions "number2 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps." Then each step corresponds to each digit\'s place. So in the case of 418, since it\'s three digits, the reasoning should have three steps, each multiplying by the ones, tens, and hundreds place digits respectively, considering their place values (i.e., 8, 10, and 400). So the steps are correct as above.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 158,422. I think that\'s solid. I don\'t see any miscalculations here. All cross-verification methods lead to the same result. So I can confidently say the product is 158,422.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 379 and 418 is \\boxed{158422}.\n']
