her approach, maybe the initial step-by-step multiplication again. \n\nOriginal multiplication:\n\n558 × 192:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n- 558 × 2 = 1,116 (correct as before).\n\n- 558 × 90 = 558 × 9 ×10. 558×9: 500×9=4,500; 58×9=522 → total 4,500+522=5,022 → times 10 is 50,220 (correct).\n\n- 558 ×100 =55,800 (correct).\n\nAdding those three: 1,116 + 50,220 = 51,336; then 51,336 +55,800 = 107,136. \n\nYes, all methods lead to the same result, so I think that's correct. \n\nWait, but in the initial reasoning, when I multiplied 558 by 90, I said 558×9 is 5,022, then adding a zero gives 50,220. That's right. \n\nSo the sum is 1,116 +50,220 +55,800. \n\nWait, adding 1,116 +50,220 first gives 51,336. Then adding 55,800 to that gives 51,336 +55,800. Let's compute that again:\n\n51,336 +55,800. \n\nAdding 51,336 +50,000 =101,336. Then add 5,800 more: 101,336 +5,800 =107,136. Yes. \n\nSo all steps check out. Therefore, the product is 107,136. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 558 and 192 is \\boxed{107136}.\n"]
