ctly.\n\nSo the reasoning steps:\n\n1. Multiply 640 by the ones digit (2) of 272: 640*2=1280\n\n2. Multiply 640 by the tens digit (70) of 272: 640*70=44,800\n\n3. Multiply 640 by the hundreds digit (200) of 272: 640*200=128,000\n\nSum all three: 1280 +44,800 +128,000 = 174,080.\n\nYes, that's correct. I think that's the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in the calculations. Let me just do one more check using calculator-like multiplication:\n\n640 \u00d7 272:\n\nFirst, 640 \u00d7 2 = 1280\n\n640 \u00d770 = 640\u00d77\u00d710=4480\u00d710=44,800\n\n640 \u00d7200=128,000. Adding those gives the same total. So I'm confident the answer is 174,080.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 640 and 272 is \\boxed{174080}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 640 and 272?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. The number 272 has three digits, so we can break down the multiplication into three steps:\n1. Multiply 640 by the ones place digit (2) of 272:\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 2 = 1280\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 640 by the tens place digit (70) of 272:\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 70 = 44800\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 640 by the hundreds place digit (200) of 272:\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 200 = 128000\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n1280 + 44800 + 128000 = 174080\n\\]\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{174080}.",
