first, then multiply by 10.\n\nLet's try that. 779 × 62:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n779 × 60 = 46,740 (since 779×6=4,674, add a zero).\n\n779 × 2 = 1,558.\n\nAdding those: 46,740 + 1,558 = 48,298. Then multiply by 10: 482,980. Same result. So that confirms it again.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using another method like the lattice method or something else, but since I'm getting the same answer through different methods, I can be more confident.\n\nWait a second, let me just do straight multiplication step by step again to be thorough.\n\nMultiplying 779 by 620:\n\nFirst, write 620 as 6 2 0.\n\nStart with the ones digit (0):\n\n779 × 0 = 0. Write down 0.\n\nNext, the tens digit (2):\n\nBut since it's the tens place, it's actually 20. So 779 × 20. As before, 779 × 2 is 1,558, so ×20 is 15,580. Write that shifted one position to the left.\n\nThen the hundreds digit (6):\n\n779 × 600. 779 ×6 is 4,674, so ×600 is 467,400. Shift two positions left.\n\nAdding all three partial products:\n\n0\n\n+15,580\n\n+467,400\n\nTotal is 482,980. Yep, same answer.\n\nTherefore, I think the product of 779 and 620 is 482,980. Let me just check once more with a calculator-style approach. Since I can't use a calculator, but perhaps approximate to see if it's reasonable.\n\n700 × 600 = 420,000.\n\nThen, 700 × 20 = 14,000.\n\n700 × 0 =0.\n\nThen 79×600 = 47,400.\n\n79×20=1,580.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n420,000 +14,000 =434,000.\n\n434,000 +47,400 =481,400.\n\n481,400 +1,580 =482,980. Exactly the same. So that's a good check.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident the answer is 482,980.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 779 and 620 is \\boxed{482980}.\n"]
