210.\n    -------.\n    .\nNow, breaking it down step by step. Since 210 has three digits: 2 (hundreds place), 1 (tens place), and 0 (ones place). Wait, actually, 210 is 2 hundreds, 1 ten, and 0 ones. So when multiplying, we'll have to multiply 930 by each of these digits, shifted appropriately, and then add them up.\n\nStarting with the ones place: the digit is 0. So multiplying 930 by 0. That should be straightforward. 930 × 0 = 0. But since it's the ones place, this result is just 0. .\n\nNext, the tens place: the digit is 1. But since it's in the tens place, it's actually 10. So we need to multiply 930 by 10. That would be 930 × 10 = 9300. However, since this is the tens place, we have to shift it one position to the left, but in the standard algorithm, we just write it as is but shifted. Wait, actually, when we do the step-by-step, each subsequent multiplication is shifted left by one digit. .\n\nWait, let me clarify. When multiplying by the tens digit (1 in the tens place), the result should be shifted one place to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 10). Similarly, when multiplying by the hundreds digit (2 in the hundreds place), it should be shifted two places to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 100). .\n\nSo let me write this out step by step:.\n\nFirst, multiply 930 by the ones digit (0):.\n\n930 × 0 = 0. Since it's the ones place, we write this as 0 (no shift).\n\nSecond, multiply 930 by the tens digit (1 in the tens place, so 10):.\n\n930 × 10 = 9300. But since it's the tens place, we shift it one position to the left, so effectively 930 × 1 (the digit) × 10 (place value) gives 9300. .\n\nThird, multiply 930 by the hundreds digit (2 in the hundreds place, so 200):.\n\n930 × 200 = 930 × 2 × 100 = 1860 × 100 = 186,000. But in the step-by-step, this would be 930 × 2 (the digit) and then shifted two places (hundreds place). .\n\nWait, maybe I should compute each step as per the standard algorithm:.\n\nFirst partial product: 930 × 0 = 0 (no shift, so