cated, but perhaps useful for verification.\n\nBut perhaps I can compute 172\u00d7800 = 137,600, 172\u00d740=6,880, and 172\u00d76=1,032. Adding them all gives the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, compute 172\u00d7846 as follows:\n\nFirst, note that 172 \u00d7 846 = 172 \u00d7 (800 + 46) = 172\u00d7800 +172\u00d746. Let me compute 172\u00d746 first.\n\n172 \u00d746: Break into 172\u00d740 +172\u00d76. Which is 6,880 +1,032 =7,912. Then add that to 172\u00d7800 (137,600). So total is 137,600 +7,912 =145,512. Same result.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 145,512.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, maybe do the multiplication vertically step by step again:\n\n       172\n\n     \u00d7846\n\n     --------\n\nFirst multiply by 6:\n\n6\u00d72=12, write 2 carryover 1.\n\n6\u00d77=42 +1=43, write 3 carryover4.\n\n6\u00d71=6 +4=10, write 0 carryover1. Wait, but wait, 172 is the top number, so when written vertically:\n\n      1 7 2\n\n    \u00d7 8 4 6\n\n    -----------\n\nFirst partial product (units digit):\n\n6\u00d72=12, write 2, carryover 1.\n\n6\u00d77=42 +1=43, write 3, carryover4.\n\n6\u00d71=6 +4=10, so write 0 and carryover 1? Wait, but since there are no more digits, so write 10? Wait, actually, when you reach the hundreds place, 6\u00d71 is 6 plus the carryover 4 gives 10, so you write 0 and carryover 1, but since there are no more digits, you have to add that 1 as a new digit. So the first partial product is 1032, which matches.\n\nSecond partial product (tens digit 4):\n\nStarting at the tens place, so we shift one position to the left. Multiply 4\u00d72=8, but since it's the tens digit, actually, it's 40\u00d72, but in the multiplication steps, we do 4\u00d72, then shift.\n\nWait, let me write it step by step:\n\nMultiply by 4 (tens place):\n\n4\u00d72 =8, write down 8.\n\n4\u00d77=28, write 8, carryover 2.\n\n4\u00d71=4 +2=6. So the partial product is 688, shifted one place left: 6880. Which is correct.\n\nThird partial product (hundreds digit 8):\n\nMultiply by 8