final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 967 and 921, we break down the multiplication step by step:\n\n1. Multiply 967 by the ones place digit (1) of 921:\n   \\[\n   967 \\times 1 = 967\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 967 by the tens place digit (20) of 921:\n   \\[\n   967 \\times 20 = 19,340\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 967 by the hundreds place digit (900) of 921:\n   \\[\n   967 \\times 900 = 870,300\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum the results of these multiplications:\n\\[\n967 + 19,340 + 870,300\n\\]\n\nFirst, add 967 and 19,340:\n\\[\n967 + 19,340 = 20,307\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to 870,300:\n\\[\n20,307 + 870,300 = 890,607\n\\]\n\nThus, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{890607}\\).', "870,300 + 20,307 = 890,607? Wait, let me check:\n\n870,300 + 20,000 is 890,300, then add 307 more: 890,300 + 307 = 890,607. Yes, that's right.\n\nWait, but let me confirm each step again because sometimes when adding large numbers, it's easy to make a mistake. Let me re-add all three numbers:\n\nFirst: 967.\n\nSecond: 19,340.\n\nThird: 870,300.\n\nAdding first and second gives 967 + 19,340 = 20,307.\n\n20,307 + 870,300.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n870,300 + 20,000 = 890,300.\n\nThen add the remaining 307: 890,300 + 307 = 890,607.\n\nHmm, seems correct. But maybe I should do the addition in another way to cross-verify.\n\nAlternatively, adding all three numbers together:\n\nStart with the thousands:\n\n870,300 is 870 thousand plus 300.\n\n20,307 is 20 thousand plus 307.\n\nSo adding 870,000 + 20,000 = 890,000.\n\nThen the remaining parts: 300 + 307 = 607.\n\nSo total is 890,000 + 607 = 890,607. Yep, same result.\n\nTherefore, the product of 967 and 921 is 890,607.\n\nWait, but just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can use another method, like the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nLet me try multiplying 967 × 921 the standard way.\n\nStarting with the ones digit of 921 (which is 1):\n\n967 × 1 = 967. Write that down.\n\nNext, the tens digit (2), but since it's the tens place, w